1
00:00:01,510 --> 00:00:13,120
 Champlain's discoveries could have resulted in an extraordinary new world empire for the French.

2
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:19,460
 Yes, but as we saw, the English Corps of Colonies was destined to become the immense territory of the United States of America.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,620
 Despite the raids of the conquistadors, who did manage to go quite a ways,

4
00:00:24,060 --> 00:00:26,980
 the Spanish could keep only a small fringe of territory.

5
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:30,970
 You've told us about the French, Spanish, English. What of the Indians?

6
00:00:31,010 --> 00:00:36,210
 Yeah, the Indians had come here much earlier. I mean, they could claim it.

7
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:39,670
 That's right, maestro. What's the Indian story?

8
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:42,360
 Who cares about a bunch of Indians?

9
00:00:42,360 --> 00:00:45,350
 I mean, it's kind of interesting.

10
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:47,850
 Now that you ask, I'll tell you about the Indians

11
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:51,850
 and what their lives were like in America in this 17th century.

12
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:55,850
 In the southwest near Spanish territories, there lived the Pueblos.

13
00:00:55,850 --> 00:01:13,540
 Dole our seas. It's those Apaches again.

14
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:30,940
 Better to cultivate their fields.

15
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:31,940
 And not steal from us.

16
00:01:31,940 --> 00:02:53,840
 Ride in.

17
00:02:53,840 --> 00:04:12,200
 She a naughty slave.

18
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:22,200
 We'd be able, the two of us, to live in peace with the Sioux where our son was born.

19
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:29,930
 We must go so many years of migration long. We are tired now my children

20
00:04:29,930 --> 00:04:36,020
 I understand you have crossed great plains and valleys and mountains an entire continent from sea to shining sea

21
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:50,060
 From sunrise in the east to sunset in the west. I'm a bit closer. You'll be power huttons like us come here now

22
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:55,800
 You needn't fear the Spanish anymore. You'll be just like us near here. There's an English village

23
00:04:55,800 --> 00:05:02,050
 Oh, yes, they too wear cloth, and they're not always kind to us when you have rested

24
00:05:02,050 --> 00:05:04,430
 I will take you to our great king, Powhatan.

25
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:08,650
 He's very wise, you'll see, and there are 200 villages like this one in his kingdom.

26
00:05:18,480 --> 00:05:30,930
 White medicine.

27
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:56,050
 The men wearing cloth first came.

28
00:05:56,130 --> 00:05:56,930
 We gave them everything.

29
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:58,060
 They were miserable.

30
00:05:58,420 --> 00:05:59,100
 We helped them.

31
00:05:59,780 --> 00:06:01,640
 Most of them almost starved to death.

32
00:06:02,270 --> 00:06:02,830
 Saved them.

33
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:06,130
 Then, little by little, reinforced, they used their weapons.

34
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:07,550
 They hurt our...

35
00:06:07,550 --> 00:06:11,610
 They went into our storehouses and stole our grain rather than work.

36
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:15,450
 Then they took our lands instead of clearing new lands.

37
00:06:15,450 --> 00:06:19,690
 If you're as bad as you say, well, why wouldn't you simply destroy them?

38
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:22,480
 Are you not a great chief with many warriors?

39
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:26,090
 No, Powahattans detest any violence.

40
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:28,940
 Before using force, we resort to cunning.

41
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:32,180
 No, Captain John Smith.

42
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:35,440
 I do, Father, and I think the man is handsome.

43
00:06:36,350 --> 00:06:39,610
 Then this is the plan we'll use, Pocahontas.

44
00:06:43,280 --> 00:06:48,180
 Every day white men cheat. They steal more of our land.

45
00:06:48,420 --> 00:06:50,520
 No, we capture them, but everyone alive.

46
00:06:50,920 --> 00:07:16,070
 Come, chief of the white men.

47
00:07:16,070 --> 00:07:20,320
 You and your brothers have all broken your word, and Chief Powahattan is pained.

48
00:07:20,380 --> 00:07:21,530
 so you will die as example.

49
00:07:21,740 --> 00:07:22,060
 Die?

50
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:22,950
 Listen.

51
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:24,110
 Well, you can't.

52
00:07:24,480 --> 00:07:25,130
 You can.

53
00:07:29,820 --> 00:07:38,230
 Pocahontas, your daughter, begs you to pardon him.

54
00:07:39,130 --> 00:07:43,080
 Oh, my child, he has betrayed our confidence and must die.

55
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:49,990
 And you swear that in the future they will cheat no more.

56
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:55,520
 Yes, I swear it, and I swear it on my own life.

57
00:07:57,130 --> 00:07:59,860
 And you may release him.

58
00:08:01,650 --> 00:08:04,020
 I vow your word.

59
00:08:04,840 --> 00:08:06,330
 Yes, Pocahontas.

60
00:08:06,350 --> 00:08:06,930
 Good, John.

61
00:08:07,540 --> 00:08:10,110
 You know, you're a pretty girl.

62
00:08:13,740 --> 00:08:17,700
 Of course, the truce didn't last long.

63
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:19,700
 When the English sent people to the colonies,

64
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:22,260
 well, they weren't always their best subjects.

65
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:23,920
 You could expect the worst.

66
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:34,450
 In the third winter in the colonies, people were starving.

67
00:08:35,060 --> 00:08:41,000
 It got so bad that finally Captain John Smith forgot all about the promises he had made.

68
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:50,230
 But Shanganu was never to forget this insult.

69
00:08:53,710 --> 00:08:58,880
 Now the colonists had made a mortal enemy, and they were to pay very dearly.

70
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:02,060
 Captain John Smith returns to England.

71
00:09:02,730 --> 00:09:05,830
 His companions promptly seize the Indian planting fields,

72
00:09:06,130 --> 00:09:09,590
 Treating these proud free people as slaves, as, as redskins.

73
00:09:10,630 --> 00:09:13,670
 Let's go, let's go faster, bunch of lazy good-for-nothings.

74
00:09:13,790 --> 00:09:14,930
 I know what you need.

75
00:09:18,290 --> 00:09:21,600
 Come, there's two of you.

76
00:09:22,490 --> 00:09:28,730
 I gave him a swig of good old pick-me-up.

77
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:32,500
 If it's really a pick-me-up, you're going to need a whole bottle.

78
00:09:35,720 --> 00:09:42,170
 Go on.

79
00:09:54,010 --> 00:09:55,590
 Chief Powhatan is dead.

80
00:09:56,790 --> 00:09:59,500
 His brother, Opichonkanu, is now the chief.

81
00:10:02,990 --> 00:10:06,090
 22nd, 1622 was to be a tragic day.

82
00:10:06,550 --> 00:10:30,800
 On that morning, 27 white people die in the St. James River.

83
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:35,520
 The terrible cycle of violence is set off with no holds barred,

84
00:10:35,580 --> 00:10:38,920
 but the new governor, Francis Wyatt, is a clever fellow.

85
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:45,690
 He invites the chief and his leaders to a powwow and personally guarantees their safety.

86
00:11:46,110 --> 00:11:49,770
 So the war against the Powhatans was to last for 14 years.

87
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:54,940
 But Opechanka knew he could wait 22 years for his revenge.

88
00:11:55,670 --> 00:11:57,140
 He was 100 years old.

89
00:11:57,810 --> 00:12:00,270
 Now, my sons, we take our revenge at last.

90
00:12:00,730 --> 00:12:03,960
 Tomorrow at dawn, we attack Jamestown.

91
00:12:08,630 --> 00:12:12,710
 Father, I'm sorry to say it, but I can't do it.

92
00:12:12,770 --> 00:12:13,970
 Please forgive me, Father.

93
00:12:16,050 --> 00:12:21,320
 For miles around, everybody talked about the Indian victory at Jamestown.

94
00:12:25,570 --> 00:12:31,660
 Ten years later, just a few miles to the north,

95
00:12:33,270 --> 00:12:35,260
 live the Delaware Indian.

96
00:12:39,110 --> 00:12:42,510
 No, don't be afraid.

97
00:12:42,630 --> 00:12:44,110
 The Swedish men are always honest.

98
00:12:47,550 --> 00:13:00,610
 Or a yellow thing.

99
00:13:00,610 --> 00:13:03,580
 You know this money that we use called wampum?

100
00:13:03,930 --> 00:13:04,710
 It's difficult to make.

101
00:13:04,810 --> 00:13:05,630
 It takes a long time.

102
00:13:05,910 --> 00:13:08,160
 But pale-face money is easy to make.

103
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:08,770
 It is?

104
00:13:09,090 --> 00:13:10,840
 Careful. Here comes the Iroquois.

105
00:13:17,660 --> 00:13:24,980
 But he gave you nothing at all.

106
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:27,040
 He gave us peace. He didn't attack us.

107
00:13:28,140 --> 00:13:29,660
 Iroquois are fierce warriors.

108
00:13:30,210 --> 00:13:32,030
 Later, this will be called a racket.

109
00:13:32,190 --> 00:13:34,930
 Only at this time in history, the word doesn't exist.

110
00:13:35,450 --> 00:13:38,600
 Hey, Pop, we be Iroquois.

111
00:13:40,250 --> 00:13:45,010
 1654, the Swedes buy Delaware and the Hinta Regents from the Indians.

112
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:47,630
 Paying for it with guns and ammunition.

113
00:13:51,040 --> 00:13:57,930
 But they were able to keep it only one year.

114
00:14:00,180 --> 00:14:14,740
 So be all right for this time, but next time I want guns or skins.

115
00:14:16,150 --> 00:14:17,190
 Why? Tell me why.

116
00:14:17,610 --> 00:14:20,570
 The Dutch give us one rifle for every ten skins.

117
00:14:21,030 --> 00:14:22,410
 They chase the Swedes out.

118
00:14:22,770 --> 00:14:25,310
 They bring much wampum from their homeland.

119
00:14:25,750 --> 00:14:27,790
 Dutch aren't friendly the way Swedes are.

120
00:14:27,870 --> 00:14:29,510
 Be careful. We're with them.

121
00:14:31,550 --> 00:14:33,320
 What is it?

122
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:34,660
 Koi.

123
00:14:38,240 --> 00:14:42,000
 Let's go.

124
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:38,510
 They're not among our friends, but you can stay with us until you find your people again.

125
00:15:38,510 --> 00:16:13,150
 We're doing an Iroquois territory.

126
00:16:17,080 --> 00:16:21,060
 But they all...

127
00:16:21,060 --> 00:16:25,950
 Don't stay here. The English and the Dutch pay well for every Algonquin scalp.

128
00:16:34,290 --> 00:16:37,150
 Adirakoi.

129
00:16:41,060 --> 00:16:45,200
 Is he holding there a weapon, is it?

130
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:47,500
 Looks more like a sort of scalp.

131
00:16:47,890 --> 00:16:49,680
 No, it's like a feather headdress.

132
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:54,630
 And just what are you people doing here?

133
00:16:54,710 --> 00:16:56,530
 We've followed the coast several moons now.

134
00:16:56,980 --> 00:16:58,770
 Come with me. I am Wampanooga.

135
00:16:59,050 --> 00:17:00,820
 I will take you to see our King Philip.

136
00:17:01,120 --> 00:17:03,280
 You see, I am his royal interpreter. This way.

137
00:17:04,010 --> 00:17:07,610
 You are welcome. We are all glad to see you, and you gladden our hearts.

138
00:17:07,610 --> 00:17:10,560
 There are great events soon to come.

139
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:13,560
 Now, come. I will show you the people of my tribe.

140
00:17:14,280 --> 00:17:18,840
 There are more and more white people here, always with more weapons, and ever more greedy.

141
00:17:19,270 --> 00:17:22,030
 Our only chance of survival is to join forces against them.

142
00:17:23,450 --> 00:17:26,290
 Philip is summoned to Plymouth by the Puritan leaders.

143
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:29,740
 Soon he realizes that he has been betrayed by his interpreter.

144
00:17:29,980 --> 00:17:32,160
 They impose unacceptable conditions.

145
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:39,380
 According to the terms, Philip is to deliver all his tribe's firearms.

146
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:42,820
 Most of the leaders refuse.

147
00:17:48,710 --> 00:17:54,210
 That was the end of the traitor.

148
00:17:56,190 --> 00:18:02,750
 You belong to us.

149
00:18:27,660 --> 00:18:44,540
 Speak to me.

150
00:18:44,540 --> 00:18:50,770
 subject of your English king I don't speak to subjects no his majesty the king to him I will

151
00:18:50,770 --> 00:20:11,170
 speak I'm even looking forward to it right idea we'll get one for all Algonquin's escaped that

152
00:20:11,170 --> 00:20:24,550
 night the English pay a high price for Algonquin scalps not too tight old friend I can't breathe

153
00:20:24,550 --> 00:20:31,460
 well you know what they say Iroquois are the strongest Iroquois are the strongest how come

154
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:41,620
 I'm not an Iroquois deacons foreigners war makes our young warriors strong and body and mind

155
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:44,190
 What are you going to do to them?

156
00:20:44,790 --> 00:20:47,350
 The men and children will take them into our tribe.

157
00:20:48,310 --> 00:20:50,480
 What about the men?

158
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:51,740
 Can't say yet.

159
00:20:52,130 --> 00:20:53,690
 First, they'll have to pass tests.

160
00:20:53,970 --> 00:20:56,870
 Depending on how they do, they could come into the tribe.

161
00:20:57,090 --> 00:20:59,610
 Or they're killed.

162
00:20:59,850 --> 00:21:00,770
 Depends on how they do.

163
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:04,460
 Look, the great council of the tribes of five nations will begin soon.

164
00:21:10,330 --> 00:21:16,780
 A great and powerful country, France, is offended by all these wars

165
00:21:16,780 --> 00:21:20,660
 that the Iroquois people are waging against our friends.

166
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:24,040
 Not to mention the harm you are causing to offer collecting.

167
00:21:24,040 --> 00:21:28,010
 If you agree to live in peace, you will be rewarded.

168
00:21:28,010 --> 00:21:33,960
 Yes, but the minute you break the peace, we'll reply with new weapons that are so terrible you'll regret it.

169
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:48,110
 In the end, the story is always the same. White men speak with forked tongue. Words last very few moons.

170
00:21:48,110 --> 00:21:51,810
 Here we are on our own land, and we're all of us united.

171
00:21:51,810 --> 00:21:56,800
 If your warriors come and dig up the hatchet and cut down the tree of peace,

172
00:21:56,800 --> 00:22:03,380
 they will find a great Iroquois nation ready and willing to defend itself.

173
00:22:03,380 --> 00:22:16,260
 They're for medicine men.

174
00:22:16,580 --> 00:22:20,020
 Only those initiated in false face rituals wear masks like this.

175
00:22:21,780 --> 00:22:23,980
 It's true we Indians like to fight.

176
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:28,180
 And because of that, the Pale Faces put one tribe against another purposely to get what they want.

177
00:22:28,260 --> 00:22:29,820
 They play with us, like children.

178
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:34,420
 And yet those first Englishmen at Jamestown wouldn't have survived without Powahattans.

179
00:22:34,930 --> 00:22:37,320
 They hunted them down and exterminated them.

180
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:38,720
 All the Powahattans.

181
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:41,660
 They completely massacred King Philip's whole tribe.

182
00:22:41,660 --> 00:22:46,160
 and yet those greedy Puritans would all have starved to death

183
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:48,660
 if the Indian tribes hadn't helped them.

184
00:22:50,450 --> 00:22:55,010
 They wiped out the Mohegans who were allies.

185
00:22:55,170 --> 00:22:56,650
 Same with the Delawares.

186
00:22:57,920 --> 00:23:00,480
 Same story with so many others.

187
00:23:00,740 --> 00:23:04,110
 Gone. All but us.

188
00:23:04,830 --> 00:23:07,200
 But why are they evil? They're people like us.

189
00:23:07,790 --> 00:23:09,210
 Are they really like us?

190
00:23:09,950 --> 00:23:13,470
 They were greedy men, surely, and some were after furs.

191
00:23:13,590 --> 00:23:15,750
 Others are hunting grounds, our lands.

192
00:23:16,350 --> 00:23:17,860
 Why were they like us?

193
00:23:17,860 --> 00:23:27,850
 I wanted to find out if an Indian baby could swim instinctively like a dog.

194
00:23:34,390 --> 00:23:40,250
 See, a while back, the French governor, François Deneuville, invited 60 of our men to a banquet.

195
00:23:41,840 --> 00:23:45,520
 Better to surprise them.

196
00:23:48,010 --> 00:23:53,590
 That's why we have to be the smartest, the strongest, the cleverest.

197
00:23:54,490 --> 00:23:58,140
 We must play on the palefaces' rivalry, not the other way around.

198
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:01,160
 Are white men really all like that?

199
00:24:01,650 --> 00:24:07,030
 I'm told in Pennsylvania, paleface William Penn is loyal to the Indians, treats them fairly.

200
00:24:07,030 --> 00:24:12,170
 I don't know how long that will go on.

201
00:24:12,170 --> 00:24:16,570
 Anyway, I don't trust any of those white creatures.

202
00:24:16,570 --> 00:24:19,470
 He reminds me of somebody I wonder whom.

203
00:24:19,470 --> 00:24:24,520
 That old boy was plenty wise because, well, a few decades later, William Penn's son didn't

204
00:24:24,520 --> 00:24:30,380
 hesitate to use any methods to rob the Indians and push them farther and farther away.

205
00:24:30,380 --> 00:24:36,000
 Well, that's what I wanted to tell you about the Indians and what it was like back then.

